command (run.sh in same dir):
#!/bin/fish
set part $argv[1]; set range $argv[2]
set p (cat prompt.txt | string collect)
set extra "THIS RUN covers only these items: $range. Your final message must be ONLY the JSONL lines (one per item, no code fences, no prose) for exactly those ids."
echo "$p

$extra" | env -i PATH=(string join : $PATH) HOME=$HOME CODEX_HOME=$HOME/.codex codex exec --ignore-user-config --ephemeral --skip-git-repo-check --sandbox read-only -m gpt-5.6-sol -c 'model_reasoning_effort="high"' -c 'web_search="live"' --json --color never -o out-$part.txt - > events-$part.jsonl 2> err-$part.txt

prompt:
You are the OpenAI-family fact-checker with web search for benchmark questions. For every item, apply its critique (use fixed_item when verdict=fix; drop verdict=reject but keep it listed with the reason), then verify each checklist point on the open web: mark each point {supported, contradicted, unverifiable} with one URL. For current-place items, verify that at least 3 real places matching the attribute exist in that city today (name + URL), without ranking them. Output per item {id, points: [{text, status, url}], item_ok: bool, note}.
Output one JSONL line per item across both files (ids cw-* and cx-*). Batch the work if needed.

Files in the current dir: claude-draft.jsonl (cw-* items), critique-of-claude-by-codex.jsonl (critiques of cw-*), codex-draft.jsonl (cx-* items), critique-of-codex-by-claude.jsonl (critiques of cx-*). Read them with the shell (read-only).
post-process: concatenated 4 outputs, split glued JSON objects (syntax only)
